From: Bedrock <bedrock@example.com>
Subject: [PATCH] x86/mm: Flush TLB when switching between threads of one mm

For deterministic execution under bedrock. A thread can be preempted between
changing PTEs and the TLB flush that follows (seen: fork in copy_page_range()
write-protecting pages, flush only at the end of dup_mmap()). A sibling thread
scheduled in that window, on the same CPU and with no CR3 load, then either
still has the old translation in the hardware TLB or does not, and takes a
page fault or not accordingly. Flush on every same-mm switch so the result no
longer depends on TLB contents.

Signed-off-by: Bedrock <bedrock@example.com>
---
 arch/x86/mm/tlb.c | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/x86/mm/tlb.c b/arch/x86/mm/tlb.c
index 5d221709353e..3d7175be5838 100644
--- a/arch/x86/mm/tlb.c
+++ b/arch/x86/mm/tlb.c
@@ -858,6 +858,19 @@ void switch_mm_irqs_off(struct mm_struct *unused, struct mm_struct *next,
 				 !cpumask_test_cpu(cpu, mm_cpumask(next))))
 			cpumask_set_cpu(cpu, mm_cpumask(next));
 
+		/*
+		 * Deterministic execution (bedrock): another thread of this mm
+		 * may have been preempted in the middle of a page-table change
+		 * whose TLB flush is still to come (fork write-protecting the
+		 * parent's pages in copy_page_range(), an mmu_gather batch, ...).
+		 * Until that flush, whether this thread still gets the old
+		 * translation depends on what the hardware TLB happens to hold,
+		 * which causes a divergence. Start every thread from an
+		 * empty TLB instead, as a switch between two mms already does.
+		 */
+		if (prev != &init_mm)
+			flush_tlb_local();
+
 		/* Check if the current mm is transitioning to a global ASID */
 		if (mm_needs_global_asid(next, prev_asid)) {
 			next_tlb_gen = atomic64_read(&next->context.tlb_gen);
-- 
2.47.3

